{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Any man of your seed in whom there is a blemish.</b> Here it says ״בּוֹ מוּם״ (\"in him a blemish\"), but below it reverses and says ״מוּם בּוֹ״ (\"a blemish in him\"), and here it says ״אֲשֶׁר יִהְיֶה״ — in the future tense, and afterward it says ״כָּל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר בּוֹ מוּם״ — in the present tense. And what reason does it give for the matter in that very verse, saying: ״כִּי כָל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר בּוֹ מוּם לֹא יִקְרַב״? And what Rashi explained — that it is not fitting that he draw near, as in ״הַקְרִיבֵהוּ נָא לְפֶחָתֶךָ\" — is not explicit in the verse." "Therefore I say that the early sages who were expert in wisdoms would know, from every blemish that developed in a man before he existed, from what sin it stemmed, which they perceived in him. By way of example: if they knew that he takes bribes, they knew of him that he would eventually come to blindness. And if they saw in him a haughty gait, they knew of him that he would eventually come to a broken leg. And the appearance of his face testified about him, for they would recognize from his facial features some evil disposition present within him that mars a particular limb, so that he is destined to develop a visible blemish — and it is fitting to remove him from the priesthood on account of what he will become. Therefore it says ״אִישׁ אִישׁ מִזַּרְעֲךָ אֲשֶׁר יִהְיֶה בוֹ מוּם״ — that the appearance of his face testified that ultimately a blemish would be in him, for from his deeds it is recognizable that he will eventually come to develop this blemish that will be in him — behold, he is disqualified on account of his future end. And it gives the reason for the matter: ״כִּי כָל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר בּוֹ מוּם״ etc. — would you not concede that if the blemish already exists in him, it is certainly not fitting that he draw near, because of ״הַקְרִיבֵהוּ לְפֶחָתֶךָ״ (Malachi 1:8)? Therefore this one too, in whom a blemish will develop in the future, is fit to be removed from the priesthood. And for this reason, when giving the reason, it did not use the language of ״יִהְיֶה״ (future) but rather ״בּוֹ מוּם״ (present). And all of this speaks of one who was not born with his blemish, for his own deeds caused the blemish to come into being, since through his deeds he marred one of his limbs. Therefore in these two verses it says ״בּוֹ מוּם״ — that his existence preceded the existence of the blemish, for the word ״בּוֹ״ speaks of the existence of the man which came first, and afterward the blemish was born in him. But below, where it says ״מוּם בּוֹ״, that speaks of one whose blemish preceded his existence — i.e., one born with it." "But we have not yet heard, if he was born with his blemish, whether the blemish disqualifies or not — for one might say that perhaps only one in whom both conditions obtain together, who has a flaw both in his soul and in his body, is fit to be removed from the priesthood so as not to offer the offering of HaShem; but one born with a blemish who has no flaw in his soul, only in his body alone — perhaps you would say it does not disqualify. Below it teaches us: ״מוּם בּוֹ אֵת לֶחֶם אֱלֹהָיו״ etc., and this speaks of one whose blemish preceded his existence, i.e., who was born with it. And its explanation is: ״כָּל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר בּוֹ מוּם מִזֶּרַע אַהֲרֹן הַכֹּהֵן לֹא יִגַּשׁ לְהַקְרִיב אֶת אִשֵּׁי ה׳״ — and even though above it already mentioned ״אֲשֶׁר יִהְיֶה בוֹ מוּם״ etc., nevertheless there it mentioned only ״לֶחֶם אֱלֹהָיו,״ and one might have thought that only the bread of Elohim — i.e., meal-offerings and the showbread — is prohibited, but other fire-offerings of HaShem are permitted to be offered. Therefore it teaches us ״לֹא יִגַּשׁ לְהַקְרִיב אֶת אִשֵּׁי ה׳״ — to equate all the fire-offerings with one who has a blemish in him. And one might have thought that this applies only to one in whom the blemish developed (בּוֹ מוּם), but not to one who was born with it (מוּם בּוֹ) — therefore it teaches us ״מוּם בּוֹ אֵת לֶחֶם אֱלֹהָיו לֹא יִגַּשׁ לְהַקְרִיב.״ And it uses ״לֶחֶם אֱלֹהָיו״ — not to exclude the fire-offerings, for all the fire-offerings have already been equated with one in whom a blemish developed, and the same applies to one born with a blemish. And it used ״לֶחֶם אֱלֹהָיו״ only because it wants to say afterward that even the bread of Elohim, which possesses greater sanctity than the offerings that go up in fire, is nevertheless permitted to be eaten from, as is written immediately after: ״לֶחֶם אֱלֹהָיו מִקָּדְשֵׁי הַקֳּדָשִׁים יֹאכֵל״ — and all the more so from the fire-offerings. And what is written ״וּמִן הַקֳּדָשִׁים יֹאכֵל״ — these are offerings of lesser sanctity, because one might have thought that since they were not permitted to a non-priest — like the meal-offerings of most holy offerings that Moses ate — so too here; and so it is stated in the Yalkut at the end of parashat Tzav (8, remez 548). And in this manner, what is also said concerning animals — sometimes ״מוּם בּוֹ״ and sometimes ״בּוֹ מוּם״ — is well settled, for all blemishes disqualify an animal, whether it was born with its blemish or the blemish developed in it afterward."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {2 "The Yalkut reference ח תקיח appears to be remez 548 in the Yalkut Shimconi parashat Tzav; the letter ח may indicate chapter 8 or simply be part of the remez notation. Resolved as (8, remez 548) with some uncertainty about the exact numbering convention intended."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:09.730039943Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}